{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q026_protein_kinase_like_function_transfer","answer":"In SCOPe 2.08, these catalytic domains share the broad class d, “Alpha and beta proteins (a+b),” the PK-like fold d.144, and—more importantly—the same PK-like superfamily d.144.1. They remain separate families: PI3K catalytic domains d.144.1.4, aminoglycoside/APH phosphotransferases d.144.1.6, and canonical protein-kinase catalytic subunits d.144.1.7. Class or fold agreement alone would establish only broad architectural similarity, not homology; membership in the same SCOPe superfamily supports remote common ancestry of the catalytic domain. It does not establish homology of entire multidomain proteins, orthology, or conserved biological function.

The defensible common mechanistic inference is a related bilobal PK-like catalytic scaffold that binds an NTP donor and positions catalytic residues, usually with a divalent metal, for phosphoryl transfer to an acceptor nucleophile. The acceptor-binding surfaces and biological contexts have diverged: canonical protein kinases recognize particular protein substrates and phosphosites; APHs recognize aminoglycosides with drug and hydroxyl-position specificity; PI3Ks recognize membrane phosphoinositides with lipid/isotype specificity. Therefore substrate class, exact substrate, phosphorylation position, inhibitor sensitivity, kinetics, and physiological role must not be transferred across these families merely from d.144.1 membership.

Specificity transfer requires substantially closer evidence: a family-level sequence relationship with conservation of catalytic and substrate-contacting residues and specificity loops; preferably several close orthologous templates rather than one remote superfamily representative; ligand- or substrate-bound structural comparison with adequate coverage and compatible active-site geometry; and direct biochemical assays against an appropriate substrate panel, including kinetic, nucleotide, and metal-dependence measurements. Active-site mutagenesis and cellular or genetic evidence should connect the reaction to the physiological function. Regulation needs separate evidence from the full-length protein and its context—domain architecture, activation-loop or other modifications, regulatory subunits, membrane recruitment for PI3Ks, allosteric interactions, oligomerization, and active/inactive-state structures. An isolated catalytic-domain template cannot safely supply those properties. Cross-family templates are suitable for modeling the conserved core, but not divergent loops, substrate pockets, regulatory interfaces, or conformational state without additional evidence.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__search_scop — /api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=d.144&mode=auto&limit=20","mcp__scope__get_scop_node — /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/56111; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/56111/annotations; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/56111/homology; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/56112; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/56112/homology","mcp__scope__list_scop_children — /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/56112/children?limit=100","mcp__scope__get_scop_node — /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/56171; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/56171/parents; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/64411; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/64411/parents; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/88854; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/88854/parents","Pretrained model knowledge — kinase catalytic mechanisms and standards for functional, regulatory, and structural-template inference"]}]}
